<section>
<p>The lesson lands hard: <strong>you cannot read causation off a table of numbers.</strong> The very same digits support opposite conclusions depending on a variable that may not even be in the spreadsheet. Which raises the question that organizes everything after this — if the data alone won't tell you, what <em>will</em>? The answer, from a computer scientist who spent the 1980s building machines that reason under uncertainty, is that you need to add something the data doesn't contain: a <strong>model of which arrows point where.</strong></p>
</section>

<section>
<p class="sec-eyebrow">The causal revolution</p>
<h2>Pearl's ladder, and the verb that changed everything</h2>
<p>Judea Pearl won the 2011 Turing Award — often called computing's Nobel — "for fundamental contributions to artificial intelligence through the development of a calculus for probabilistic and causal reasoning." His central image, popularized in <em>The Book of Why</em> (2018), is a <em class="term">Ladder of Causation</em>{% tip 'Pearl’s ladder separates association, intervention, and counterfactual reasoning into increasingly demanding levels.' %} with three rungs, each demanding a stronger kind of question and a stronger kind of evidence than the one below. Most of statistics and nearly all of machine learning, Pearl likes to needle, never leaves the bottom rung.</p>
<p>Climb it yourself:</p>
</section>

