<section>
<h3>The do-operator: seeing is not doing</h3>
<p>Here is the conceptual hinge of the entire modern field, and it is worth saying slowly. There are two very different things you can do with a variable X.</p>
<p>You can <strong>condition</strong>{% tip 'Conditioning means narrowing attention to cases where some variable already has a given value.' %} on it — written <code>P(Y | X = x)</code>. This means: among all the cases where X <em>happened</em> to equal x, what's the distribution of Y? You're filtering an existing population. This is <em>seeing</em>.</p>
<p>Or you can <strong>intervene</strong> — written, in Pearl's notation, <code>P(Y | do(X = x))</code>. This means: reach in, <em>force</em> X to equal x for everyone, snipping X away from whatever usually causes it, and then watch Y. This is <em>doing</em> — it's what a randomized experiment performs.</p>
<p>When there's a confounder, these two numbers come apart, and the gap between them <em>is</em> the bias. Among people who <em>happen</em> to buy lots of ice cream, drowning really is more common (because they're the summer people) — so the <em>seeing</em> quantity is high. But if you <em>force</em> a random sample of people to buy ice cream in, say, evenly distributed weather, drownings don't budge — the <em>doing</em> quantity is flat. The machine below lets you turn a confounder up and down and watch the two quantities diverge.</p>
</section>

